  // A-checks every 500 airframe hours, C-checks every 4,000. Interval/cost/condition figures are
  // shared across both playstyles - they run the same cycle at the same cost, because what differs
  // between them is how long a check grounds the aircraft, not what the work costs. Only
  // aCheckDowntimeHours/cCheckDowntimeHours
  // differ, and come from each playstyle's own "maintenance" override block below.
  // aCheckCost/cCheckCost are DELIBERATE GAME-BALANCE FIGURES, not real A/C-check bills (which run
  // into six and seven figures) - sized to stay a genuine but affordable hit against a casually
  // flown airline's accumulated monthly surplus by the time the hours come due. Do not "correct"
  // them toward realistic figures for the same reason the starter lease rate isn't corrected: a
  // real A-check bill runs into six figures on its own, which would make casual play a losing
  // position again. These are deliberate game-balance numbers, labelled so nobody "fixes" them.
  "maintenance": {
    "aCheckIntervalHours": 500,
    "cCheckIntervalHours": 4000,
    "aCheckCost": 45000,
    "cCheckCost": 320000,
    "conditionDecayPerHour": 0.12,
    "aCheckConditionRestorePercent": 35
  },
  // Buying a used airframe: cheap to buy, expensive to run.
  // Shared across playstyles: the used-aircraft market doesn't depend on which playstyle the buyer
  // chose. priceMultiplier 0.55 = 45% cheaper than new; the saving is repaid by starting 70% of the
  // way into both maintenance cycles and 30 condition points below a fresh airframe.
  "usedAircraft": {
    "priceMultiplier": 0.55,
    "startingHoursSinceACheckFraction": 0.7,
    "startingHoursSinceCCheckFraction": 0.7,
    "startingConditionPercent": 70,
    "startingAirframeHours": 18000
  },
  // Selling an owned aircraft, which must lose money on the spread - if an aircraft sold for what
  // it cost, buying and selling would be a free round trip and cash would stop meaning anything.
  // Shared across playstyles: what a worn airframe is worth on the secondhand market
  // doesn't depend on how realistically the SELLING airline chose to be billed elsewhere. Anchored,
  // not guessed - with these defaults a used aircraft bought at exactly usedAircraft's starting
  // state (70% condition, 70% into both maintenance cycles) and immediately resold values at
  // exactly 0.50 of the playstyle's new price, strictly below the 0.55 it cost to buy, so even a
  // used-aircraft flip loses money. newAircraftResaleFactor alone (0.80) already makes a
  // buy-new-sell-immediately round trip a loss before any wear is applied.
  "depreciation": {
    "newAircraftResaleFactor": 0.80,
    "conditionDepreciationWeight": 0.30,
    "cycleWearDepreciationWeight": 0.30,
    "groundedForMaintenancePenalty": 0.05,
    "minResaleFactor": 0.10
  },
  // Moving an idle aircraft to another airport the airline already serves, without flying it there -
  // the fix for an airframe stranded somewhere with nothing useful to do. Shared across playstyles:
  // this is a flat service charge for a positioning move, not a game-balance lever like the starter
  // lease rate beside it, and the figure below is the user's own (2026-08-13). Flat rather than
  // distance-scaled on purpose - pricing by distance would make the worst strandings the least
  // affordable to fix. It does bite harder in Casual (~2/3 of a typical sector's profit) than in
  // True-life; if that ever needs correcting, add per-playstyle override blocks rather than
  // hardcoding a figure in the endpoint. Repositioning is a PLAYER-ONLY action - an aircraft
  // available to virtual pilots must be reserved for the player first (see
  // AircraftRepositionEvaluator.RepositionRefusal.NotReservedForPlayer).
  "aircraftRepositioning": {
    "cost": 2000
  },
  // Shared across both playstyles - the founding pilot's salary is already a realistic figure and
  // needs no game-balance treatment, unlike the starter lease rate beside it. Everything else that
  // differs by playstyle (starting capital, lease deposit term, starter lease rate, insurance)
  // lives in the "casual"/"trueLife" blocks below. A playstyle is a named set of overrides,
  // not a code path: EconomyConfigCatalog resolves one fully-merged EconomyConfig per playstyle at
  // load time, and nothing downstream branches on which one an airline chose.
  "airlineStartup": {
    "startingPilotMonthlySalary": 9000
  },
  "casual": {
    // Exactly what Chunk D shipped - unchanged by the playstyle split. Starting capital, the
    // 1-month lease deposit and the game-balance starter lease/insurance are what let one aircraft
    // flown casually (~1 leg/day) be genuinely profitable - which it has to be, or the second
    // aircraft never arrives and the game has no progression at all. DELIBERATE GAME-BALANCE FIGURES,
    // NOT REAL-WORLD RATES - do NOT "correct" leaseRates.A320/B738 or monthlyInsurancePerAircraft
    // toward realistic numbers, that reopens the unplayable-grind problem the 2026-08-08 rebalance
    // fixed. leaseDepositMonths itself is a pacing decision, not a fictional-number label - a
    // single month's deposit is a normal real-world lease term too.
    "airlineStartup": {
      // 60,000 covers the 30,000 lease deposit plus a month's cushion, and - the load-bearing part -
      // sits BELOW the price of a used airframe (~51,200), so a new airline cannot buy past the
      // progression loop before flying a single sector. This was 2,000,000 while Casual purchase
      // prices were still realistic and therefore unreachable; once they came down to ~93,100 new,
      // that same figure bought 39 used A320s on day one and the earn-your-second-aircraft loop
      // stopped existing. Starting lean is safe here precisely because one leg a day already nets
      // about 45,000/month - the airline is profitable from month one, so runway matters far less
      // than the temptation it removes.
      "startingCapital": 60000,
      "leaseDepositMonths": 1.0
    },
