  // The modest VATSIM online-flying bonus (G12) - a small revenue uplift plus a small reputation
  // nudge for a sector FSOps itself corroborated as genuinely flown online (see
  // VatsimFlightCorroborationService), never merely from a CID being configured. Shared across
  // playstyles - see VatsimOnlineBonusConfig's own doc in EconomyConfig.cs for the derivation of
  // each figure. 0.03 revenue / 0.15 reputation-alpha are deliberately small: offline flying stays
  // fully rewarding with no VATSIM account at all, and this is a bonus on top of an already-earned
  // ordinary sector, not a second economy.
  "vatsimOnlineBonus": {
    "revenueUpliftFraction": 0.03,
    "reputationAlphaMultiplier": 0.15,
    "minimumOnlineFraction": 0.5
  },
  // Contract flying - jobs offered by other operators, flown personally in an aircraft the operator
  // supplies. Shared across playstyles: a contract is somebody else's aeroplane and somebody else's
  // costs, so how realistically the player chose to have their OWN airline billed says nothing about
  // what a third party would pay them.
  //
  // The two figures worth understanding before retuning anything:
  //
  //   feePerLeg exists so that five 300 nm sectors pay more than one 1,500 nm sector. They ARE more
  //   work - five departures, five approaches, five sessions - and a fee driven by distance alone
  //   would say they are identical. It is also what stops a light single's ocean crossing, which is
  //   the most distinctive thing in the app, being the worst-paid job on the board.
  //
  //   abandonChargeFraction is 1.0 because that is the figure the user stated: "if someone does 3
  //   legs when there are 2 legs remaining they would get charged for the remaining 2 legs." The
  //   fraction being described IS the unflown share, so scaling it down again would be a second
  //   fraction nobody asked for. It reads harsher than it plays: earn three legs, pay for two, and
  //   abandoning most of the way through lands near break-even - "you gained nothing for the
  //   evening" rather than a trap. Stopping at leg one costs real money, and it should, because that
  //   is where the aeroplane ends up in the worst possible place.
  //
  //   The safety valve is not this number. Nothing at all is charged if no leg was ever flown,
  //   because the justification the charge rests on - somebody has to recover the aircraft - is
  //   simply false when the aircraft never left. Handing an untouched job back is free at any
  //   fraction, so accepting a contract is never a decision the player cannot undo.
  //
  // scaleBands is the setting that decides whether the board is worth browsing at all. The weights
  // are relative, and the spread is the point: a forty-minute domestic hop should sit next to a
  // multi-leg ocean crossing. Making them equal, or narrowing the distances, produces a board where
  // every job is the same size - which is the same failure as a generator that always offers four
  // legs.
  "contracts": {
    "boardSize": 8,
    "boardRefreshHours": 24,
    "deadlineDays": 28,
    "abandonChargeFraction": 1.0,
    "baseFee": 900,
    "feePerNm": 3.10,
    "feePerLeg": 850,
    "feePerPayloadKg": 0.55,
    "feePerPassenger": 90,
    "minimumFee": 750,
    "destinationDistanceTolerance": 0.35,
    "categoryMultipliers": {
      "LightSingle": 0.55,
      "LightTwin": 0.75,
      "UtilityTurboprop": 1.00,
      "BusinessJet": 1.35,
      "RegionalAirliner": 1.55,
      "Narrowbody": 2.10,
      "Widebody": 3.20
    },
    "kindMultipliers": {
      "Ferry": 1.10,
      "Cargo": 1.00,
      "Charter": 1.05
